          "settings": {
            "title": "Choosing Your Bike",
            "intro": "",
            "question_1": "Kodiak vs Kodiak V2 — which should I buy?",
            "answer_1": "Choose the Kodiak if you want the classic bamboo-platform cargo bike, a slightly lighter bike, and a rigid fork that stays predictable under load ($2,049 CAD). Choose the Kodiak V2 if you want front suspension, an integrated battery, a torque sensor, and fatter 20\" tires — especially if you ride rough pavement or want the optional passenger / front-basket setup ($2,149 CAD).\n\nQ2: Can these replace a second car?\nA2: For many households, yes — for trips under about 10–15 km with cargo that fits the rack. You still need a car for long highway trips, large furniture, or multi-stop days beyond battery range. The point is cutting the short, expensive trips.\n\nQ3: Is the Kodiak V2 good for a passenger?\nA3: It can be fitted with optional rear passenger bars with padded seats (rear child carrier listed at $200; front rack $80). Accessories are sold separately. Always follow weight limits and use spoke protection / child-safe setups where required.",
            "question_2": "Can these replace a second car?",
            "answer_2": "For many households, yes — for trips under about 10–15 km with cargo that fits the rack. You still need a car for long highway trips, large furniture, or multi-stop days beyond battery range. The point is cutting the short, expensive trips.",
